{"ratings":[{"id":"C1","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Ordinary log lines that are also valid unnumbered TAP results can exhaust the excerpt limit and hide an actual assertion failure."},{"id":"C2","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A sufficiently verbose failing test can lose its assertion and name from implementer feedback, requiring further investigation or a rerun."},{"id":"C3","consequence":"nuisance","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Bun's recap creates duplicate excerpts and a misleading omission notice even when all original failure diagnostics were retained."},{"id":"C4","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Failed checks with excerpts can hide previously visible troubleshooting output from the land log and artifact view, costing investigation time."},{"id":"C5","consequence":"secret","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A credential printed in an early failure can be persisted in new excerpts, and clipping can leave a fragment that later literal redaction misses."},{"id":"C6","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"An evaluation case containing a failed gate result with excerpts is rejected by the strict schema and cannot load."},{"id":"C7","consequence":"diag","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Near the excerpt limit, land formatting can truncate diagnostic text or a test name and produce a broken omission notice."}]}